  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe airmass has dried a little since yesterday, with the main\ndrying observed in the low levels. That is resulting in less \ninstability, with MLCAPE less than 500 J/kg in all but the far \neastern plains. While lapse rates will still be conducive to \nscattered showers and storms, they will be high based and capable \nof producing gusty outflow winds to 40-55 mph as DCAPE grows to \n1200-1400 J/kg over the plains and I-25 Corridor. There is still a\nconditional threat of a couple severe storms over the northeast \nplains this evening, as low level moisture is forecast to start \nadvecting westward from Nebraska. However, that airmass could be \nmore capped. If they develop or move southward from Nebraska, \nthen a threat of large hail and damaging winds would exist. The \nSPC Slight Risk area looks reasonable there. Only an isolated \nhigh wind gust threat would exist over the rest of the plains late\nthis afternoon and early evening.\n\nA weak frontal boundary attempts to push southward across the \nplains late tonight and Tuesday, with an uptick in low level \nmoisture. While some of this gets mixed out during peak heating\nespecially along the I-25 Corridor, instability and moisture \nvalues are both forecast to increase from today. We expect \nscattered showers and storms to develop over the mountains around \nthe noon hour, and then spread and intensify as they move eastward\ninto the higher instability airmass over the eastern plains. A \ncouple storms may become severe east of the I-25 Corridor where \ninstability grows to 1000-2000 J/kg as one heads east to the \neastern border. \n\nFor Wednesday, low level moisture is expected to increase further\nand pool back closer to the Front Range and I-25 Corridor due to \npersistent but light easterly flow behind an additional frontal \nsurge. While temperatures will be a little cooler (one day finally\nnear normal!), we do expect to build sufficient instability for \nour highest chance of strong and potentially a couple severe \nstorms closer to the I-25 Corridor. Storms will also be capable of\nlocally heavy rainfall given the increase in low level moisture. \n\nThursday is still expected to see another round of scattered \nshowers and storms in the afternoon, but the ridge axis begins to\ntilt toward the forecast area. We'll still have sufficient \ninstability over the plains for a couple strong/severe storms, as \nlong as we can break the cap. The odds of storms decreases by \nFriday as we see the ridge axis shifting across the forecast area,\nwith a drier and more capped airmass. There's still uncertainty \nabout how fast we scour the eastern plains moisture, so we can't \ntotally rule out a few storms. Temperatures will almost certainly \nbe warming up, with mid 90s the most likely outcome across the \nplains by Friday as the ridge/thermal axis shifts overhead. \n\nMore uncertainty enters the picture for the weekend, but ensembles\ndo support more heat. We'll also be getting into a little stronger\nwest/southwest component aloft, as a significant shortwave moves \neast across the Northern Rockies. This would support increasing \nbreezes and an increase of elevated fire weather conditions for \nthe northern mountains. However, at the same time most ensemble \nmembers keep mid/high level moisture streaming off the Central  \nPacific, so humidity numbers might stay up sufficiently and also \nbe supportive of a few showers and storms. Saturday looks quite \nhot again (mid 90s for the plains and I-25 Corridor), while \nensembles start to suggest some cooling by Sunday or Monday.",
